  </figure>
</div>

<p>Robert "Lucky Bob" St. Henry, whose real name was W. C. Shaefer, was born in Nebraska in the 1880s. Described by relatives as a "free spirit," he was an early American barnstormer who lived in several places around the country. {% person "glenn-curtiss" %}Glenn Curtiss{% endperson %}, founder of the aircraft company that bore his name, sponsored a flying team that included Shaefer and toured the country to promote Curtiss aircraft designs.</p>

<p>The 1911 fair also included more traditional entertainment, agricultural, and athletic events. The Cole Carnival Company, which had arrived Friday night, pitched its tents just west of the electric plant on Main Street, the site of today's Papa John's Pizza. Shortly after a special train arrived from Bismarck at noon with several hundred passengers, the carnival band led the way to the fairgrounds. Admission was 50 cents, and the grandstand was packed for each day's attractions. The crowd cheered during baseball games in which Glen Ullin defeated Bismarck on Saturday and Mandan defeated New Salem on Sunday.</p>

<p>The directors of the Missouri Slope Fair and Agriculture Association entered the exhibition backed by a $1,000 guarantee from Mandan businessmen. Receipts totaled $1,670 from the airship exhibition, $55 from concessions, and $81 from the carnival. From the grand total of $1,806, St. Henry and his assistants were paid $1,282. After approximately $100 in miscellaneous expenses, the fair association made a profit of about $400.</p>
